{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788174350467,"sessionID":"ses_fa8815f1bffeeE6hC19uqKKiGR","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a0577f36c57d7380aff8d8a186e22d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/Ori/Documents/Projects/BunkerGame/docs/director/ART-BIBLE.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/Ori/Documents/Projects/BunkerGame/docs/director/ART-BIBLE.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # THE OUTSKIRTS — Art Bible\n2: \n3: ## Visual Goal\n4: \n5: THE OUTSKIRTS should present a polished, atmospheric survival world with a\n6: deliberate visual language. The final bunker must not look like plain cubes,\n7: default Roblox presentation, or an arbitrary collection of props.\n8: \n9: ## Bunker Mood\n10: \n11: The starting bunker is abandoned but functional. It should feel creepy,\n12: industrial, aged, and believable without losing navigational clarity.\n13: \n14: The starting bedroom should eventually communicate this through elements such\n15: as:\n16: \n17: - an aged bed\n18: - locker or storage\n19: - shelving\n20: - believable bunker architecture\n21: - pipes and cables\n22: - industrial details\n23: - cobwebs\n24: - grime and wear\n25: - a flickering fluorescent fixture\n26: - emergency or utility lighting\n27: - a later usable torch/flashlight\n28: - a strong visual route toward the bunker interior\n29: \n30: These are desired art-pass elements, not authorization to add them during a\n31: layout or composition task.\n32: \n33: ## Environment Construction Direction\n34: \n35: Future art passes should pursue:\n36: \n37: - believable structural thickness\n38: - layered materials\n39: - trim and transitions between surfaces\n40: - beams and supports\n41: - pipes\n42: - cables and conduits\n43: - vents\n44: - industrial fixtures\n45: - purposeful clutter\n46: - wear, grime, and age\n47: - environmental storytelling\n48: - controlled lighting\n49: - strong silhouettes\n50: - deliberate color palette\n51: - high-quality composition\n52: \n53: Architecture should read as constructed space, not thin planes or hollow cubes.\n54: Detail should reinforce purpose, history, scale, and navigation.\n55: \n56: ## Composition Principles\n57: \n58: - Give every major space a clear purpose and focal hierarchy.\n59: - Use corridors, thresholds, lighting, and silhouettes to guide the player.\n60: - Balance enclosed tension with readable circulation.\n61: - Keep important routes legible even when the environment becomes detailed.\n62: - Compose props as part of the room's function and history, not as scattered\n63:   decoration.\n64: - Use repetition and a controlled material/color family to make the bunker\n65:   cohesive.\n66: \n67: ## Existing Art Status\n68: \n69: The current bunker is functional reference, not final art. Its pavement shell,\n70: asset geometry, materials, colors, and placement must not dictate the rebuild.\n71: \n72: The previous `BedroomBlockout` is visually rejected. Its design and composition\n73: are not approved.\n74: \n75: ## Phase Discipline\n76: \n77: ### Design/Composition Phase\n78: \n79: Allowed:\n80: \n81: - spatial planning\n82: - room relationships\n83: - circulation\n84: - world placement\n85: - scale and silhouette studies\n86: - neutral blockout categories\n87: \n88: Not allowed:\n89: \n90: - final textures\n91: - decals\n92: - cobwebs\n93: - detailed clutter\n94: - final materials\n95: - final lighting\n96: - VFX\n97: - art polish\n98: \n99: ### Art Pass\n100: \n101: An art pass begins only after the director approves the layout and composition.\n102: Art direction does not authorize gameplay implementation.\n\n(End of file - total 102 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# THE OUTSKIRTS — Art Bible\n\n## Visual Goal\n\nTHE OUTSKIRTS should present a polished, atmospheric survival world with a\ndeliberate visual language. The final bunker must not look like plain cubes,\ndefault Roblox presentation, or an arbitrary collection of props.\n\n## Bunker Mood\n\nThe starting bunker is abandoned but functional. It should feel creepy,\nindustrial, aged, and believable without losing navigational clarity.\n\nThe starting bedroom should eventually communicate this through elements such\nas:\n\n- an aged bed\n- locker or storage\n- shelving\n- believable bunker architecture","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/Ori/Documents/Projects/BunkerGame/docs/director/ART-BIBLE.md","text":"# THE OUTSKIRTS — Art Bible\n\n## Visual Goal\n\nTHE OUTSKIRTS should present a polished, atmospheric survival world with a\ndeliberate visual language. The final bunker must not look like plain cubes,\ndefault Roblox presentation, or an arbitrary collection of props.\n\n## Bunker Mood\n\nThe starting bunker is abandoned but functional. It should feel creepy,\nindustrial, aged, and believable without losing navigational clarity.\n\nThe starting bedroom should eventually communicate this through elements such\nas:\n\n- an aged bed\n- locker or storage\n- shelving\n- believable bunker architecture\n- pipes and cables\n- industrial details\n- cobwebs\n- grime and wear\n- a flickering fluorescent fixture\n- emergency or utility lighting\n- a later usable torch/flashlight\n- a strong visual route toward the bunker interior\n\nThese are desired art-pass elements, not authorization to add them during a\nlayout or composition task.\n\n## Environment Construction Direction\n\nFuture art passes should pursue:\n\n- believable structural thickness\n- layered materials\n- trim and transitions between surfaces\n- beams and supports\n- pipes\n- cables and conduits\n- vents\n- industrial fixtures\n- purposeful clutter\n- wear, grime, and age\n- environmental storytelling\n- controlled lighting\n- strong silhouettes\n- deliberate color palette\n- high-quality composition\n\nArchitecture should read as constructed space, not thin planes or hollow cubes.\nDetail should reinforce purpose, history, scale, and navigation.\n\n## Composition Principles\n\n- Give every major space a clear purpose and focal hierarchy.\n- Use corridors, thresholds, lighting, and silhouettes to guide the player.\n- Balance enclosed tension with readable circulation.\n- Keep important routes legible even when the environment becomes detailed.\n- Compose props as part of the room's function and history, not as scattered\n  decoration.\n- Use repetition and a controlled material/color family to make the bunker\n  cohesive.\n\n## Existing Art Status\n\nThe current bunker is functional reference, not final art. Its pavement shell,\nasset geometry, materials, colors, and placement must not dictate the rebuild.\n\nThe previous `BedroomBlockout` is visually rejected. Its design and composition\nare not approved.\n\n## Phase Discipline\n\n### Design/Composition Phase\n\nAllowed:\n\n- spatial planning\n- room relationships\n- circulation\n- world placement\n- scale and silhouette studies\n- neutral blockout categories\n\nNot allowed:\n\n- final textures\n- decals\n- cobwebs\n- detailed clutter\n- final materials\n- final lighting\n- VFX\n- art polish\n\n### Art Pass\n\nAn art pass begins only after the director approves the layout and composition.\nArt direction does not authorize gameplay implementation.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":102,"totalLines":102,"truncated":false}},"title":"BunkerGame/docs/director/ART-BIBLE.md","time":{"start":1788174350451,"end":1788174350463}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_01a0577f36c57d7380aff8d8a186e22d"}},"id":"prt_0577f38690018SqnCSw2gbT9Cv","sessionID":"ses_fa8815f1bffeeE6hC19uqKKiGR","messageID":"msg_0577f3206001qXZCV5keFldUfy"}}
