{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788174353132,"sessionID":"ses_fa8815f1bffeeE6hC19uqKKiGR","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a0577f42097592b260309312242e5e","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/Ori/Documents/Projects/BunkerGame/docs/director/BUNKERGAME_ENVIRONMENT_ART_BIBLE.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/Ori/Documents/Projects/BunkerGame/docs/director/BUNKERGAME_ENVIRONMENT_ART_BIBLE.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # BunkerGame Environment Art Bible\n2: \n3: ## Authority and Scope\n4: \n5: **Status:** Human Director approved  \n6: **Approval scope:** Gate 1 spatial/compositional reference only  \n7: **Authoritative reference:** `Workspace.SOL_GATE1_GOLDEN_REFERENCE`, version `v36-focused-reveal-candidate`  \n8: **Durable artifact:** `artifacts/sol-gate1-golden-reference/SOL_GATE1_GOLDEN_REFERENCE.build.luau`  \n9: **Required evidence:** `artifacts/sol-gate1-golden-reference/evidence/reveal-pass/`\n10: \n11: v36 is mandatory teaching evidence for spatial design. It is **not** a\n12: finished-art reference. Production must reproduce its spatial logic, not copy\n13: its present vegetation, lighting, materials, path finish, or architectural\n14: detail level.\n15: \n16: ## 1. Golden Spatial Rules\n17: \n18: These rules are mandatory for production Gate 1.\n19: \n20: 1. **One coherent landform.** The approach, shoulders, bunker burial, and rear\n21:    ridge must read as connected geology—not separate mounds or assembled ramps.\n22: 2. **Terrain-driven dogleg.** The primary route must make a meaningful lateral\n23:    displacement and heading change because of the landform.\n24: 3. **Delayed visibility.** The bunker entrance must not be clearly readable\n25:    from the initial approach or immediately before the dogleg.\n26: 4. **Reveal beat.** After the direction change, the entrance becomes the\n27:    primary read; the route is secondary and the enclosing ridge is tertiary.\n28: 5. **Buried facility.** The bunker reads as a fortified opening cut into a\n29:    mountain. Most roof and side-shell mass remains concealed.\n30: 6. **Direct terrain contact.** Earth meets retaining structure, portal frame,\n31:    and exposed construction edges without a visible gap or a dirt canopy placed\n32:    over a freestanding box.\n33: 7. **Player-scale traversal.** Route width, grade, camera clearance, entrance\n34:    clearance, and turning space must work at Roblox avatar scale.\n35: 8. **Asymmetry.** One side supplies stronger terrain/structural framing while\n36:    the other supplies counter-space. Do not mirror the reveal.\n37: 9. **Depth layers.** Player views must contain intentional foreground framing,\n38:    a midground entrance/reveal, and a background ridge or landform continuation.\n39: 10. **Terrain dominance.** Even at the reveal, the hillside remains the largest\n40:     visual mass. Architecture strengthens the discovery without exposing the\n41:     complete bunker shell.\n42: \n43: ## 2. Future Visual Quality Targets\n44: \n45: Every later gate must substantially exceed v36. Do not copy its placeholder\n46: finish.\n47: \n48: - **Vegetation:** authored forest clusters, species/age/scale variation,\n49:   believable understory, controlled negative space, stronger tree assets, and\n50:   no repeated silhouette rhythm.\n51: - **Route surface:** erosion, runoff, compression, edge breakup, embedded\n52:   stones/roots, material transitions, and terrain pressure that make the route\n53:   feel formed rather than painted.\n54: - **Bunker architecture:** credible structural thickness, retaining and\n55:   drainage logic, deeper entrance sequencing, construction joints, functional\n56:   infrastructure, and architecture appropriate to a serious buried facility.\n57: - **Materials:** coherent high-fidelity ground, rock, concrete, metal, dampness,\n58:   wear, and contact transitions with consistent scale and restrained variation.\n59: - **Lighting and atmosphere:** survival-horror focal hierarchy, exterior to\n60:   interior adaptation, readable darkness, atmospheric depth, controlled color\n61:   relationships, and practical lighting rather than light spam.\n62: - **Storytelling and polish:** purposeful weathering, damage, maintenance\n63:   history, utilities, and restrained clutter. Every detail must explain\n64:   function, history, navigation, or threat.\n65: \n66: The following v36 qualities are explicitly **not** approved final targets:\n67: grass, tree assets/repetition, vegetation diversity, path finish, lighting,\n68: sky/atmosphere, material fidelity, bunker detail, weathering/storytelling, and\n69: overall polish.\n70: \n71: ## 3. Anti-Patterns\n72: \n73: Failed Iterations 01–05 established these rejection conditions:\n74: \n75: - terrain balls, rounded blob chains, disconnected mounds, stacked slabs, or\n76:   staircase wedges presented as a hillside;\n77: - trench or box walls used in place of a continuous landform;\n78: - a bunker box with dirt placed around or on top of it;\n79: - exposed roof/side shell, flat facade, shallow doorway, or arbitrary primitive\n80:   bunker massing;\n81: - a straight approach that frames the entrance early;\n82: - a fake rectangular occluder instead of terrain and route direction;\n83: - a flat geometric path strip laid over the ground;\n84: - mirrored retaining structures or centered bilateral composition;\n85: - uniformly distributed, evenly spaced, or obviously repeated vegetation;\n86: - disconnected retaining pieces, floating geometry, clipping, weak grounding,\n87:   or visible terrain/architecture gaps;\n88: - clutter, props, lighting effects, or weathering used to disguise unresolved\n89:   massing;\n90: - overhead/debug views used as primary art evidence;\n91: - technical QA, worker opinion, or metric compliance presented as visual\n92:   approval.\n93: \n94: ## 4. Objective Checks\n95: \n96: MiMo must report these checks with current evidence. They are necessary, but\n97: they do not replace Art Director or Human Director review.\n98: \n99: ### Spatial and Traversal\n100: \n101: - Record the production route polyline and its segment headings. Demonstrate at\n102:   least one deliberate heading change of **35 degrees or more** before reveal.\n103: - Verify a continuous traversable route width of at least **12 studs** through\n104:   the approach and dogleg.\n105: - Sample route surface height along the centerline; report maximum grade and any\n106:   abrupt step. No accidental step may exceed normal avatar traversal.\n107: - Verify at least **7.5 studs** of clear entrance height and player-camera\n108:   clearance along the intended path.\n109: - Raycast from the initial approach and pre-dogleg cameras to the entrance focal\n110:   point. Terrain must occlude both sightlines.\n111: - Raycast from the reveal camera to the entrance focal point. The intended\n112:   portal/recess must be reachable without an unintended blocker.\n113: - Confirm no route mesh/part floats above or clips through terrain. If the route\n114:   uses parts or meshes, sample and report surface-to-terrain offsets.\n115: - Report the number and bounds of exposed bunker exterior components. Provide\n116:   player views proving that roof and side shell remain substantially concealed.\n117: \n118: ### Required Visual Evidence\n119: \n120: Capture fresh, uncropped gameplay-FOV views:\n121: \n122: 1. initial player-height approach;\n123: 2. player-height before dogleg;\n124: 3. player-height reveal moment;\n125: 4. player-height entrance/scale view;\n126: 5. player-relevant three-quarter composition;\n127: 6. side silhouette showing burial and ridge continuity.\n128: \n129: Add close evidence for any suspected floating, clipping, transition, collision,\n130: or camera-clearance defect. Do not substitute an elevated overview.\n131: \n132: ### Source, Persistence, and Safety\n133: \n134: - `FILESYSTEM_SOURCE=PASS`\n135: - `ROJO_MAPPING=PASS`\n136: - `ROJO_BUILD=PASS`\n137: - `ROJO_SYNC=PASS`\n138: - `LIVE_EDIT_VERIFICATION=PASS`\n139: - `PERSISTENCE_AFTER_RECONCILIATION=PASS`\n140: - Production gameplay anchors and protected systems remain present and\n141:   unchanged unless the active task explicitly authorizes a change.\n142: - Studio is verified back in Edit mode after every runtime check.\n143: \n144: ### Visual Authority\n145: \n146: MiMo may issue `TECHNICAL_QA=PASS`. MiMo may not issue\n147: `VISUAL_QA=PASS`, `ART_DIRECTION=PASS`, or\n148: `DIRECTOR_REVIEW=PASS`. Metrics can identify defects; only direct inspection\n149: of current player-relevant evidence can approve composition and visual quality.\n\n(End of file - total 149 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# BunkerGame Environment Art Bible\n\n## Authority and Scope\n\n**Status:** Human Director approved  \n**Approval scope:** Gate 1 spatial/compositional reference only  \n**Authoritative reference:** `Workspace.SOL_GATE1_GOLDEN_REFERENCE`, version `v36-focused-reveal-candidate`  \n**Durable artifact:** `artifacts/sol-gate1-golden-reference/SOL_GATE1_GOLDEN_REFERENCE.build.luau`  \n**Required evidence:** `artifacts/sol-gate1-golden-reference/evidence/reveal-pass/`\n\nv36 is mandatory teaching evidence for spatial design. It is **not** a\nfinished-art reference. Production must reproduce its spatial logic, not copy\nits present vegetation, lighting, materials, path finish, or architectural\ndetail level.\n\n## 1. Golden Spatial Rules\n\nThese rules are mandatory for production Gate 1.\n\n1. **One coherent landform.** The approach, shoulders, bunker burial, and rear","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/Ori/Documents/Projects/BunkerGame/docs/director/BUNKERGAME_ENVIRONMENT_ART_BIBLE.md","text":"# BunkerGame Environment Art Bible\n\n## Authority and Scope\n\n**Status:** Human Director approved  \n**Approval scope:** Gate 1 spatial/compositional reference only  \n**Authoritative reference:** `Workspace.SOL_GATE1_GOLDEN_REFERENCE`, version `v36-focused-reveal-candidate`  \n**Durable artifact:** `artifacts/sol-gate1-golden-reference/SOL_GATE1_GOLDEN_REFERENCE.build.luau`  \n**Required evidence:** `artifacts/sol-gate1-golden-reference/evidence/reveal-pass/`\n\nv36 is mandatory teaching evidence for spatial design. It is **not** a\nfinished-art reference. Production must reproduce its spatial logic, not copy\nits present vegetation, lighting, materials, path finish, or architectural\ndetail level.\n\n## 1. Golden Spatial Rules\n\nThese rules are mandatory for production Gate 1.\n\n1. **One coherent landform.** The approach, shoulders, bunker burial, and rear\n   ridge must read as connected geology—not separate mounds or assembled ramps.\n2. **Terrain-driven dogleg.** The primary route must make a meaningful lateral\n   displacement and heading change because of the landform.\n3. **Delayed visibility.** The bunker entrance must not be clearly readable\n   from the initial approach or immediately before the dogleg.\n4. **Reveal beat.** After the direction change, the entrance becomes the\n   primary read; the route is secondary and the enclosing ridge is tertiary.\n5. **Buried facility.** The bunker reads as a fortified opening cut into a\n   mountain. Most roof and side-shell mass remains concealed.\n6. **Direct terrain contact.** Earth meets retaining structure, portal frame,\n   and exposed construction edges without a visible gap or a dirt canopy placed\n   over a freestanding box.\n7. **Player-scale traversal.** Route width, grade, camera clearance, entrance\n   clearance, and turning space must work at Roblox avatar scale.\n8. **Asymmetry.** One side supplies stronger terrain/structural framing while\n   the other supplies counter-space. Do not mirror the reveal.\n9. **Depth layers.** Player views must contain intentional foreground framing,\n   a midground entrance/reveal, and a background ridge or landform continuation.\n10. **Terrain dominance.** Even at the reveal, the hillside remains the largest\n    visual mass. Architecture strengthens the discovery without exposing the\n    complete bunker shell.\n\n## 2. Future Visual Quality Targets\n\nEvery later gate must substantially exceed v36. Do not copy its placeholder\nfinish.\n\n- **Vegetation:** authored forest clusters, species/age/scale variation,\n  believable understory, controlled negative space, stronger tree assets, and\n  no repeated silhouette rhythm.\n- **Route surface:** erosion, runoff, compression, edge breakup, embedded\n  stones/roots, material transitions, and terrain pressure that make the route\n  feel formed rather than painted.\n- **Bunker architecture:** credible structural thickness, retaining and\n  drainage logic, deeper entrance sequencing, construction joints, functional\n  infrastructure, and architecture appropriate to a serious buried facility.\n- **Materials:** coherent high-fidelity ground, rock, concrete, metal, dampness,\n  wear, and contact transitions with consistent scale and restrained variation.\n- **Lighting and atmosphere:** survival-horror focal hierarchy, exterior to\n  interior adaptation, readable darkness, atmospheric depth, controlled color\n  relationships, and practical lighting rather than light spam.\n- **Storytelling and polish:** purposeful weathering, damage, maintenance\n  history, utilities, and restrained clutter. Every detail must explain\n  function, history, navigation, or threat.\n\nThe following v36 qualities are explicitly **not** approved final targets:\ngrass, tree assets/repetition, vegetation diversity, path finish, lighting,\nsky/atmosphere, material fidelity, bunker detail, weathering/storytelling, and\noverall polish.\n\n## 3. Anti-Patterns\n\nFailed Iterations 01–05 established these rejection conditions:\n\n- terrain balls, rounded blob chains, disconnected mounds, stacked slabs, or\n  staircase wedges presented as a hillside;\n- trench or box walls used in place of a continuous landform;\n- a bunker box with dirt placed around or on top of it;\n- exposed roof/side shell, flat facade, shallow doorway, or arbitrary primitive\n  bunker massing;\n- a straight approach that frames the entrance early;\n- a fake rectangular occluder instead of terrain and route direction;\n- a flat geometric path strip laid over the ground;\n- mirrored retaining structures or centered bilateral composition;\n- uniformly distributed, evenly spaced, or obviously repeated vegetation;\n- disconnected retaining pieces, floating geometry, clipping, weak grounding,\n  or visible terrain/architecture gaps;\n- clutter, props, lighting effects, or weathering used to disguise unresolved\n  massing;\n- overhead/debug views used as primary art evidence;\n- technical QA, worker opinion, or metric compliance presented as visual\n  approval.\n\n## 4. Objective Checks\n\nMiMo must report these checks with current evidence. They are necessary, but\nthey do not replace Art Director or Human Director review.\n\n### Spatial and Traversal\n\n- Record the production route polyline and its segment headings. Demonstrate at\n  least one deliberate heading change of **35 degrees or more** before reveal.\n- Verify a continuous traversable route width of at least **12 studs** through\n  the approach and dogleg.\n- Sample route surface height along the centerline; report maximum grade and any\n  abrupt step. No accidental step may exceed normal avatar traversal.\n- Verify at least **7.5 studs** of clear entrance height and player-camera\n  clearance along the intended path.\n- Raycast from the initial approach and pre-dogleg cameras to the entrance focal\n  point. Terrain must occlude both sightlines.\n- Raycast from the reveal camera to the entrance focal point. The intended\n  portal/recess must be reachable without an unintended blocker.\n- Confirm no route mesh/part floats above or clips through terrain. If the route\n  uses parts or meshes, sample and report surface-to-terrain offsets.\n- Report the number and bounds of exposed bunker exterior components. Provide\n  player views proving that roof and side shell remain substantially concealed.\n\n### Required Visual Evidence\n\nCapture fresh, uncropped gameplay-FOV views:\n\n1. initial player-height approach;\n2. player-height before dogleg;\n3. player-height reveal moment;\n4. player-height entrance/scale view;\n5. player-relevant three-quarter composition;\n6. side silhouette showing burial and ridge continuity.\n\nAdd close evidence for any suspected floating, clipping, transition, collision,\nor camera-clearance defect. Do not substitute an elevated overview.\n\n### Source, Persistence, and Safety\n\n- `FILESYSTEM_SOURCE=PASS`\n- `ROJO_MAPPING=PASS`\n- `ROJO_BUILD=PASS`\n- `ROJO_SYNC=PASS`\n- `LIVE_EDIT_VERIFICATION=PASS`\n- `PERSISTENCE_AFTER_RECONCILIATION=PASS`\n- Production gameplay anchors and protected systems remain present and\n  unchanged unless the active task explicitly authorizes a change.\n- Studio is verified back in Edit mode after every runtime check.\n\n### Visual Authority\n\nMiMo may issue `TECHNICAL_QA=PASS`. MiMo may not issue\n`VISUAL_QA=PASS`, `ART_DIRECTION=PASS`, or\n`DIRECTOR_REVIEW=PASS`. Metrics can identify defects; only direct inspection\nof current player-relevant evidence can approve composition and visual quality.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":149,"totalLines":149,"truncated":false}},"title":"BunkerGame/docs/director/BUNKERGAME_ENVIRONMENT_ART_BIBLE.md","time":{"start":1788174353106,"end":1788174353120}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_01a0577f42097592b260309312242e5e"}},"id":"prt_0577f42c9001B6O2S8vCn6SvYO","sessionID":"ses_fa8815f1bffeeE6hC19uqKKiGR","messageID":"msg_0577f3ba0001w4Nm3QNCtSwziq"}}
